t's when I started  mapping out a really rough composition  which honestly was weird because I was  also drawing something with the  knowledge that a decent chunk of it will  be covered by logos titles and headers  for the games casing  however after drawing landscape  thumbnails on YouTube for god knows  how long drawing in portrait felt fresh  and offered a lot in the way of  composition and for those that don't  know composition is how you orientate or  format your drawing or artwork think of  it as the blueprint for a house a  composition layout is sort of the  blueprint for the final art piece and so  I started doodling  I began by outlining the shapes I wanted  for this cover where certain characters  would be and what I would be putting  where because it's a game cover we are  creating here and not just a nice piece  of artwork I needed to be conscious of  where the title for the game would be  such that it wouldn't obstruct anything  important and that the eye would be  drawn to it I gotta sell those copies  after all thankfully a lifetime of  making youtube thumbnails prepared me  for such a problem I put a nice big  semicircle at the top who knows I might  put a moon there or something I mean it  does hold significance with Goku's life  after all it is part of his Saiyan  ancestry however since the game is  called Kakarot I just didn't think one  Goku on the cover would be enough  there's always being two sides to the  character at least a kind innocent side  and another that gets very serious with  all is on the line with this in mind I  opted to scribble down onto the canvas  a crude kid Goku and his Super Saiyan  transformation as fitting  representations of this characters  duality I swear I'll draw better than  this later once this outstanding display  of drawing humility is over with and I  have any idea of where the hell I'm  going with this I move on to the sketch  phase using this as my basis for  composition from here on in as you've  probably already noticed in addition to  drawing an outstanding kid Goku earlier  I also drew his space pod the idea I had  was to have on one cover a beginning and  an end for the character I wanted to  draw his first moments outside the  capsule after landing on earth confused  and apprehensive with his older more  serious Super Saiyan form roaring behind  him confidently to draw  Kakarot I used Dragon Ball super bro Li  when referencing the armor the pose  however came from the top of my head  once I was satisfied with the sketch I  began sketching the pot he arrived again  using reference of the craft from Dragon  Ball super broadly in order to  understand how the inside worked and I  grabbed a picture from Google Images all  at all this little guy was fun and a  nice change of pace enjoyed the typical  Dragon Ball adult characters I always do  with that said now it's time to draw the  typical adult Dragon Ball characters I  always do as the second pair of this  cover art I wanted the innocence of the  younger Goku to be in direct contrast  with the older more serious side of  himself while following the initial  composition blueprint I began drawing a  front view of a Super Saiyan Goku old  pretty standard stuff tried to get the  face right spent some time in the jaw  and face shape drawing his ears in and  his neck however there was one minor  problem the drawing was trashed and  needed to be destroyed  needless to say starting over wasn't  exactly my favorite thing to do after  dedicating a decent chunk of time to  this drawing but this is Dragon Ball Z  Kakarot dad it deserves my best so I  started again this time trying a  slightly more difficult angle but it is  one of my favorites from a frontal view  and I'm happy to announce that I was  much much happier with how the head  turned out for this one that being said  I still had to draw all of the body and  I'm sure that won't take forever I have  to spend in what felt like four hours  referencing tons of real-life muscle  groups I decided to call it a day with  the body considering most of it will be  obscured by a small child with a  spaceship anyway which makes my inner  artist cry but these are the sacrifices  we make in the name of progress right as  part of the next step I shed a tear as I  hide the screaming Saiyans perfect ABS  behind the young armored Goku when  something terrible comes to my attention  the composition doesn't work at all  predominantly because the kid Goku and  the pudgy caiman are standing in  three-quarter pose while the screaming  Super Saiyan is fronting something needs  to change here and while I love this  cute little Goku sketch there's  absolutely no way I'm discarding the  screaming Super Saiyan that easily took  me two hours to fully render which means  you guessed it I have to discard this  little Goku ski  and start from scratch yes I'm not  kidding but I mean who needs sleep my  dad always said real gold doesn't fear  trial-by-fire  so here I go marching into the flames  burning the candle at both ends  since I had already drawn Goku with his  armor on I decided to draw Goku from the  very first chapter of Dragonball without  it instead and that's what I had an idea  for a really fun visual so the first  thing I'm gonna do is make sure that  this image of kid Goku is drawn front  done no more composition mistakes this  time but one thing I did want to do was  to recreate the praying pose Goku  assumed at the beginning of the story  with the four star ball and with the  color scheme I have in mind I think it's  gonna look awesome I hope in truth  discarding the last kid Goku sketch was  upsetting but this new one I'm actually  much happier with both from a  compositional perspective and an  artistic one he just looked so damn cute  and I love this idea of him praying to  the Dragon Ball it really helps tie the  whole piece together and works well  within the composition it being  perfectly in line with the two other  heads straight down the middle of the  canvas drawing his little hands in  prayer was a little tricky at first but  I settled on a pose I was happy with  eventually I checked the final  composition with the Super Saiyan  illustration and I got it works so now  that we're happy with the sketching  composition we move on to the most  satisfying part the line art this was  probably the most time-consuming part in  isolation despite it not requiring much  thought at all I did fix and change a  few things as I went through with the  anatomy additionally in areas that  lacked detail like the mouth I took the  time to add the appropriate detail to  the roof the tongue and the teeth I  played around with the idea of adding  veins to his face also you know  reminiscent of the original animes  transformation but in the end I thought  it was going a little overboard and so I  opted to move forward without them  however the biggest task had to be the  hair so it's just as well hair is my  favorite thing to draw on a character  Dragon Ball has a lot of different ways  to draw hair the Lego looks solid color  wavy but I instead of going the routes a  lot of modern Dragonball likes to take I  try to make each bunching of hair assume  its own unique shape consistency along  with random strands poking out here and  there like regular hair tends to do I  love the results this approach heals and  it's the way I almost always draw my  hair I'm not trying to recreate our  mimic a style that said it's not exactly  a time sensitive practice  and the light alone took well over an  hour to finish with the hair I rechecked  the composition to make sure everything  is lining up correctly moment of truth  time I really don't want to redraw  anything and thank God it works but one  tiny little thing is still bothering me  about this now it's not a big deal and  thankfully it's an easy fix you see that  little tail there  well that tail unbalances the entire  piece you see there's nothing on the  other side of the image to  counterbalance that tail so let's add  something I still have to draw the  shredded pants and shirt bits from the  Super Saiyan Goku illustration so I  figured the belt would be a fitting way  to fill up that negative space and  surprise surprise it doli was the  tattered clothes look about right but  really this whole thing really comes  together when we add color in the  comments under my original tweet folks  seem to like the red backdrop of the  original piece so let's keep that now  what inspired the color pad I want to  use for this piece came from an idea I  had for the Dragon Ball itself instead  of going with traditional colors I  wanted it to glow but instead of showing  you the entire process of me gloriously  coloring it seriously it took about four  hours I want you to get the full effect  on first viewing so three two one as you  can see the glow I use for the Dragon  Ball I mirrored with the glow of the  Super Saiyan hair I use nice and warm  colors for the skin tones and the  clothes on both characters I also added  these veins of light to the Dragon Ball  that I thought added this mystical  quality to it there are dozens of layers  at work for the color alone and the  entire piece has close to a hundred  layers total my computer might have  crashed a number of times getting to  this point but we are not done while  looking at this you might have noticed  that there is a little pink glow on alt  Goku shoulders and arms that's because  we have one more thing to add that ties  this entire piece together  unfortunately because I'm an idiot I  forgot to record this part but I do have  the line art and the color layers for  you guys to check out in isolation  essentially I wanted a villain to look  ominously from behind and who better of  your lid for Goku than Frieza he is a  long history with the character and is  the reason for his being sent to earth  in the first place I ended up drawing  his face in full but in reality I only  needed his eyes and a pink load to be  emitted from them to offset the yellows  and oranges from the main piece I know I  said I wanted to include the moon  the beginning but I think this works  better here and there you have it now is  the time for the moment of truth where  we add the logos age warnings branding  and game case header to reveal the final  piece I give you my interpretation of  the Dragon Ball Z Kakarot video game box  art no I'm going to sleep huh if you  like this artwork don't forget to leave  a like and subscribe for more thank you  so much for watching  [Music]

